{"parentUuid":"ce0bb374-8af8-47a0-ab13-23eec8b36afd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a1a61d0d62fa7dbf3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY32Uto71fSdN4yBsJNh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Yaab8HQEJTpa7vgiNnqsm6","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-1/adjudication.md","content":"# Adjudication, round 1\n\nScope: the 13 partially accepted findings in `for-adjudication.md`, judged against the current `data/*.json` and `docs/00-design-principles.md`. `scripts/check.py` reports 0 errors and 0 warnings (1154 capabilities, 143 skills, 100 contracts, 15 critics).\n\n### K-COMPLETE-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `PLAT.PAL.process` now reads \"... HTTP(S)/TLS client (platform stacks)\" (owner platform-architect, contributor security-engineering), and the `C-PAL` summary names \"HTTP(S)/TLS client\". `NET.TRANS.web` covers WebSocket and WebRTC data channels, with WebTransport where available. `CORE.TYPES.crypto` (\"Vetted cryptographic primitives wrapper, CSPRNG, per-platform selection\", containers-core-types, contributor security-engineering) is exposed through `C-TYPES`. `XC.SEC.crypto-policy` (\"Cryptography & TLS/certificate policy\", security-engineering) exists. Adjacent concerns: `RES.IO.remote` (HTTP/CDN IO with cache and offline policy), `RES.IO.errors` (retries) and `BLD.REL.on-demand` (background assets).\n- Ruling rationale: The HTTPS/TLS client, the browser transports, the crypto primitives with CSPRNG and the TLS/certificate policy each now have exactly one owner and a contract path. Putting the client in the PAL is technically sound, because console certification requires the platform network stacks. Keeping primitives in a foundation (L1) skill, with policy owned by security, follows the §4 policy-vs-implementation pattern. Non-binding note: the HTTP client is a clause inside a capability about processes and pipes, and proxies and certificate pinning are not named anywhere. Splitting it into its own `PLAT.PAL.http` line would help phase-2 research seeding, but the ownership gap the critic raised is closed.\n\n### K-FUTURE-10 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: xr-runtime now owns `PLAT.XR.passthrough` (E), `PLAT.XR.scene` (M, contributors collision-detection and navigation-pathfinding), `PLAT.XR.anchors` (M, contributors network-architect and persistence-save), `PLAT.XR.depth-occlusion` (M, contributor render-architect), `PLAT.XR.layers` (E), `PLAT.XR.gaze-input` (M, contributor input-system), `PLAT.XR.light-estimation` (M) and `PLAT.XR.mobile-ar`. The radar entry \"Mixed reality & spatial computing\" carries an owner, a revisit trigger and a VR-only fallback. `C-XRVIEW` covers compositor layers. xr-runtime owns 13 capabilities, below `MAX_CAPS_SKILL` = 30.\n- Ruling rationale: Every capability the critic proposed exists with the proposed owner and contributors, and the anchor-sharing boundary with networking is expressed through the contributor. The critic made the split conditional on exceeding the size norm, and xr-runtime does not exceed it. Non-binding note: the xr-runtime `purpose` and `expertise` still describe VR only (OpenXR, stereo rendering, motion-to-photon). They should gain scene understanding, spatial anchors and passthrough compositing, so that G2 can judge the \"distinct literature load\" the builder names as the split trigger.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-4 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-VIEW` (L2, owner spatial-transforms) summary: \"view sources (gameplay camera, cinematic, editor, photo mode, XR pose) with priority/blend stack and shake/FOV channels, per-local-player views, view origin for LWC rebasing; derived listener and streaming-source signals\". `WLD.SPACE.views` has contributors gameplay-systems-toolkit, cinematics-sequencer, xr-runtime and render-architect. `C-VIEW` is consumed by render-architect, cinematics-sequencer, xr-runtime, world-architect, audio-architect, gameplay-systems-toolkit and world-editor-viewport, and optionally by gameplay-architect. `C-XRVIEW` and `C-SIGNIF` require `C-VIEW`. `GAM.SYS.camera` now reads \"(C-VIEW sources)\". `AUD.ARCH.listeners` (\"Multiple listeners & split-screen mixing\") exists, and `C-AUDIO` now covers multiple listeners. Accessibility reaches the channels through `UI.A11Y.motion` and the process contract `C-A11Y`, which gameplay-systems-toolkit and cinematics-sequencer consume.\n- Ruling rationale: The arbitration layer exists with one owner, and every sink the critic listed consumes it. Placing it at L2 below gameplay is technically better than a gameplay-owned `C-CAMERA`, because the editor, XR and cinematics must drive views in configurations without the gameplay framework. Non-binding note: the spatial-transforms `purpose` and `expertise` do not yet mention view arbitration or camera blending.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-15 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `C-AIAGENT` exists (L4, owner ai-behavior-perception; \"budget, timeout, moderation and authored fallback\"), and gameplay-architect consumes it as `C-AIAGENT?`. `GAM.AI.learned` and `GAM.AI.llm` are now M and reference `C-AIAGENT`. The `GAM.AI.llm` line also references `XC.SEC.genai`. ai-behavior-perception consumes `C-ML?` and `C-LIVE?`. `C-LIVE` covers \"generative/inference service calls with quotas\". The following guardrail capabilities exist: `XC.SEC.genai` (with PII), `QA.CERT.genai`, `PLAT.SVC.genai-boundary` and `PLAT.SVC.moderation`. Runtime TTS is `AUD.CONTENT.speech`. The radar entry has an owner, a revisit trigger and a fallback. Still missing:\n  - No capability or contributor covers localization or culturalization of runtime-generated text. localization-i18n appears on no genai-related capability.\n  - Nothing covers persistence of conversational memory. ai-behavior-perception does not consume `C-SAVE?`, and no capability names this concern.\n  - ai-behavior-perception `expertise` is still [\"game AI\", \"utility theory\", \"HTN planning\"], with no ML or LLM competence.\n  - `check.py` has no rule for the §7 claim that non-established techniques are \"reached through optional contracts\". The radar check (lines 309–320) verifies that non-E capabilities are tracked and have a revisit trigger. It does not check fallback, and it does not check reachability.\n- Ruling rationale: The core extension point and the moderation, budget and fallback requirements from 06 A12 are now in place. However, the critic listed specific concerns as having no owner, and two of them still have none: localization of generated text and persistence of conversational memory (which also carries PII). The competence gap in the skill's expertise also remains. The builder's statement that the radar rule replaces the proposed check is technically wrong. The radar rule tests tracking. The critic's rule tests dependency optionality, which the radar rule never examines. So §7's reachability claim remains unchecked.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add localization-i18n as a contributor to `GAM.AI.llm` or `XC.SEC.genai`. Alternatively, add `UI.LOC.generated` (\"Locale/culturalization of runtime-generated text\"), owner localization-i18n, contributor ai-behavior-perception.\n  2. Add `C-SAVE?` to ai-behavior-perception `consumes`, and add persistence-save as a contributor to `GAM.AI.llm`. Name the behavior \"conversational memory persistence and retention\", or cover it in `C-AIAGENT`'s summary.\n  3. Add \"ML policy/LLM integration\" to ai-behavior-perception `expertise`.\n  4. Close the §7 gap in one of two ways. Either add a check.py rule that a skill owning an M, X or S capability consumes every ML or service contract (`C-ML`, `C-MLGPU`, `C-RT`, `C-LIVE`) only as optional `?`, or require radar entries for M, X and S capabilities to include `fallback`. Otherwise, reword §7 so that it does not claim a check that does not exist.\n\n### K-LEGACY-7 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: The §6 row now reads \"submission strategy is chosen per hardware tier by ADR ... CPU-culled batched path is first-class on TBDR/lite3d. 2D draws through batched draw lists by design\". `RND.ARCH.submission-strategy` exists (render-architect, contributors geometry-pipeline and platform-mobile, \"TBDR suitability\"). `RND.GEO.cpu-submission` exists (\"first-class tier path (own budgets and tests)\", contributor platform-mobile). `RND.GEO.visbuffer` requires a TBDR suitability ADR. geometry-pipeline has profiles [lite3d, std3d], and its `purpose` names both paths. Legacy patterns L12 and L27 are qualified. Two items are unchanged. geometry-pipeline `expertise` is still [\"GPU culling\", \"mesh shaders\", \"ExecuteIndirect\"]. The K-RENDER critic check is still the unqualified \"no draw-call-centric or DX11-era assumptions\".\n- Ruling rationale: Keeping both paths in one skill because they serve one contract (`C-INSTANCES`) is a legitimate choice, and the per-tier decision now sits with render-architect. However, the critic's stated root cause was that the mobile path is \"owned by an agent whose expertise and critic checks push the other way\". The builder's decision to keep the same owner is sound only if that owner's competence covers the CPU path. It does not, and the domain critic's check can still be read as flagging the now first-class path.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add \"CPU culling & instanced batching\" and \"TBDR/mobile GPU submission best practice (Mali/Adreno/Apple)\" to geometry-pipeline `expertise`.\n  2. Change the K-RENDER check to \"no draw-call-centric or DX11-era assumptions on capable tiers; CPU-batched submission on TBDR/lite3d follows RND.ARCH.submission-strategy\".\n\n### K-PERF-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: The new skill runtime-scalability (expert, parent core-runtime-architect, kind runtime, profiles all) owns `CORE.SCALE.governor` (contributors frame-orchestration, platform-mobile, performance-architect), `CORE.SCALE.actuators` (\"dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, workers\"), `CORE.SCALE.profiles` and `CORE.SCALE.autodetect`. It provides `C-SCALE` (L1, universal runtime; \"actuator registration and governor control signals\"), so every runtime actuator owner reaches it implicitly. It consumes `C-INSTR` for frame and GPU timings and `C-PAL` for thermal and memory-pressure events. `PRF.METH.scalability` is narrowed to policy. performance-architect is `kind: process`.\n- Ruling rationale: One closed-loop governor with a single actuator registry now exists, and it drives the actuators the critic named. The builder's owner change is required, not a preference: performance-architect is now a process skill, and process skills cannot own runtime code. The residual split between the thermal loop and `PLAT.MOB.thermal` is ruled on under K-SYSTEMS-13.\n\n### K-PROD-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: The `C-ORCH` summary now contains \"write sets by module convention (src/<skill-id>/, tools/<skill-id>/; tests owned by the test-owning skill); shared-file protocol; contributor semantics (review + change request, never direct writes)\". `ARCH.ORG.write-sets` exists (program-orchestration, contributor build-system-toolchains). The shared hotspots the critic named have owners:\n  - `ARCH.STRUCT.registration`: declarative self-registration with \"no central tables\", which removes most shared registration files.\n  - `RND.SHADER.interop`: shared shader headers, owned by shader-system.\n  - `BLD.SYS.codegen`: codegen integration.\n- Ruling rationale: Write sets keyed by skill id are disjoint by construction, because check.py enforces unique skill ids. That delivers the proof the critic asked for without per-skill globs. The contributor semantics now answer the critic's question directly. Deferring explicit globs until code exists (M0) is a legitimate choice with no material risk, because the convention already makes write sets unambiguous.\n\n### K-PROD-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: All 143 skills have a `workstream`, with 15 values. `ARCH.ORG.staffing` (\"co-hosting of skills per organization scale (by workstream)\") and `ARCH.GOV.process-tiers` both exist. §5 line 79 describes organizational scale-down. The check.py configuration report now counts \"build skills\" only (runtime and tool) and excludes the 22 process skills: indie-2d-client has 75 build skills and minimal-client has 66.\n- Ruling rationale: Organizational scale-down and governance weight are now owned capabilities. The workstream field serves as the co-hosting key that the critic's `host_group` would have provided. Runtime membership is reported separately from process membership. Defining the solo, small and large tier tables is the deliverable of `ARCH.ORG.staffing`, so deferring `host_group` to that capability is a legitimate choice.\n\n### K-QUALITY-6 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `QA.STRAT.contracts` is narrowed to \"Contract-test framework & consumer-driven test policy\". `C-TEST` now includes \"conformance suites owned by contract owners with consumer-driven additions\". All 86 non-P contracts carry `\"conformance\": true`. However, `scripts/check.py` and `scripts/model.py` contain no reference to `conformance` (grep returns nothing). The flag is therefore not checked, although the disposition says \"(checked)\". `C-ORCH` contains no rule that a provider change must pass the consumer-contributed tests.\n- Ruling rationale: Ownership is resolved in substance, and a separate owner field would indeed be redundant. But one of the two claimed changes is missing. With no guard, a flag that is always `true` carries no information, and a new contract can omit it silently. The critic's point about replaceability also depends on a provider change being gated by consumer-driven tests, and that gating rule is not stated.\n- Required change:\n  1. In `check.py`, add `E(f\"code contract {cid} has no conformance suite\")` for every contract with layer ≠ \"P\" that lacks `conformance: true`. Add a mutation case for it to `selftest()`.\n  2. Extend the `C-TEST` or `C-ORCH` summary with \"a provider change merges only when the contract's conformance suite, including consumer-contributed cases, passes\".\n\n### K-RENDER-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: texture-streaming-vt has profiles [\"all\"]. `RND.TEX.formats` and `RND.TEX.mip-streaming` carry no profile gate. `RND.TEX.vt` and `RND.TEX.feedback` are gated to std3d, and `RND.TEX.ntc` to aaa. The min2d description is now \"Small 2D games (sprite/tile renderer, 2D physics)\" and no longer claims 2.5D. The new base profile lite3d (\"mobile, portable, 2.5D, stylized; CPU-submitted rendering tier\") includes geometry-pipeline with `RND.GEO.cpu-submission`, direct-lighting-shadows and translucency-decals. `PHY.DYN.dof-lock` (\"Planar / DOF-locked simulation (2.5D)\") belongs to rigid-body-dynamics, which has the lite3d profile. check.py configuration closure passes.\n- Ruling rationale: The critic accepted both alternatives the builder combined: drop 2.5D from min2d and give it a proper profile, and give basic textures an owner in every profile with VT as a tier. Capability-level profile gating reaches the same scale-down as splitting the skill. min2d now carries only formats and mip streaming, not VT.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-13 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `CORE.SCALE.governor` and `CORE.SCALE.actuators` exist under runtime-scalability. `PLAT.PAL.power` (\"Power, thermal & performance-hint APIs\", platform-architect, contributor runtime-scalability) exists. The runtime-scalability `expertise` includes ADPF and Apple thermal state. Two gaps remain:\n  - `PLAT.MOB.thermal` still reads \"Thermal & power adaptivity\". It is owned by platform-mobile, a runtime skill whose `purpose` still says \"thermal/power adaptivity\".\n  - The registered actuators are \"dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, workers\". Frame-rate cap and refresh-rate selection are not among them, and neither is P/E-core placement. Frame-rate and refresh-rate selection live in `PLAT.MOB.frame-pacing` and CORE.FRAME, and placement lives in `CORE.JOBS.priorities`. job-system-task-graph is not a contributor to `CORE.SCALE.actuators`.\n- Ruling rationale: The owner choice is sound: one skill holds both the registry and the loop. The critic's concrete failure mode was \"dynres, job placement and thermal handling run three uncoordinated control loops\". The data still gives a second runtime skill a capability worded as thermal adaptation, so two agents can each legitimately build a thermal control loop. The frame-rate cap, the primary thermal actuator in ADPF guidance and on handhelds, and P/E placement, both of which the critic named, are not registered as governor actuators.\n- Required change:\n  1. Rename `PLAT.MOB.thermal` to \"Mobile thermal/power signal backends (ADPF thermal headroom, performance-hint sessions) feeding CORE.SCALE.governor; no independent control loop\", and update the platform-mobile `purpose` to match.\n  2. Change `CORE.SCALE.actuators` to \"(frame-rate cap/refresh selection, dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, worker count & P/E placement)\". Add frame-orchestration, platform-mobile and job-system-task-graph as contributors.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-BUDGET` is now layer \"P\", universal \"all\", owner performance-architect (\"Budget numbers per hardware tier × configuration × refresh class ...; perf gate thresholds\"). `C-SCALE` is L1, universal runtime, owner runtime-scalability, requires `C-CFG`. runtime-scalability's parent is core-runtime-architect, and it is placed in milestone M0. performance-architect has `kind: process` and `targets: []`. check.py reports no foundation cycle.\n- Ruling rationale: The split is implemented as the critic proposed. Assigning `C-SCALE` to a dedicated expert under core-runtime-architect instead of the lead is consistent with the lead/expert structure. It also keeps the governor logic from K-PERF-8 and K-SYSTEMS-13 with the contract owner.\n\n### K-TOOLS-12 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `ED.WORLD.blockout` (\"Blockout & in-editor modeling (primitives, booleans, UVs)\", world-editor-viewport, contributor asset-cook-processors) exists. `ED.WORLD.ld-utilities` exists. `CNT.COOK.geometry-ops` (\"Mesh boolean, remesh & UV-unwrap operations for processors and blockout\", asset-cook-processors, contributor world-editor-viewport) exists. However, world-editor-viewport consumes [\"C-EDCMD\", \"C-EDHOST\", \"C-WORLD\", \"C-RSCENE\", \"C-SPATIAL\", \"C-VIEW\"] and has empty `tool_consumes`. asset-cook-processors `provides` nothing, and it plugs into `C-COOK`, which world-editor-viewport does not consume. The `C-MATH` booleans (`CORE.MATH.compgeo`) are not consumed by world-editor-viewport either. world-editor-viewport `purpose` and `expertise` (\"3D manipulation UX\", \"large-world editing\") do not mention blockout or modeling.\n- Ruling rationale: The missing workflows now exist as capabilities, and deferring a separate mesh-modeling skill is legitimate. But the builder's placement splits one interactive feature across two skills with no contract between them. The blockout tool cannot call the geometry operations it depends on except by reaching into another skill's internals, which the contracts rule forbids (`contracts.json` `_doc`: \"Skills depend on contracts, never directly on another skill's internals\"). check.py does not detect this, because contributor links are not dependencies.\n- Required change:\n  1. Give world-editor-viewport a contract path to the geometry operations. One option: add `C-COOK` to its `tool_consumes` and extend the `C-COOK` summary with \"interactive, editor-invoked geometry operations (boolean/remesh/UV)\", building on the existing \"per-asset on-demand invocation\". The other option: have world-editor-viewport consume `C-MATH` and reword `CNT.COOK.geometry-ops` so that the interactive kernels are `CORE.MATH.compgeo`.\n  2. Add blockout and in-editor modeling to world-editor-viewport `purpose`, and add \"mesh editing / geometry processing\" to its `expertise`.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:** 13 findings adjudicated: 8 UPHOLD (K-COMPLETE-3, K-FUTURE-10, K-GAMEPLAY-4, K-PERF-8, K-PROD-2, K-PROD-8, K-RENDER-7, K-SYSTEMS-14), 5 OVERTURN (K-GAMEPLAY-15, K-LEGACY-7, K-QUALITY-6, K-SYSTEMS-13, K-TOOLS-12).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3241,"cache_read_input_tokens":109629,"output_tokens":8437,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":596},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3241},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8437,"cache_read_input_tokens":109629,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3241,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3241,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Yaab8HQEJTpa7vgiNnqsm6":{"file_path":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-1/adjudication.md","content":"# Adjudication, round 1\n\nScope: the 13 partially accepted findings in `for-adjudication.md`, judged against the current `data/*.json` and `docs/00-design-principles.md`. `scripts/check.py` reports 0 errors and 0 warnings (1154 capabilities, 143 skills, 100 contracts, 15 critics).\n\n### K-COMPLETE-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `PLAT.PAL.process` now reads \"... HTTP(S)/TLS client (platform stacks)\" (owner platform-architect, contributor security-engineering), and the `C-PAL` summary names \"HTTP(S)/TLS client\". `NET.TRANS.web` covers WebSocket and WebRTC data channels, with WebTransport where available. `CORE.TYPES.crypto` (\"Vetted cryptographic primitives wrapper, CSPRNG, per-platform selection\", containers-core-types, contributor security-engineering) is exposed through `C-TYPES`. `XC.SEC.crypto-policy` (\"Cryptography & TLS/certificate policy\", security-engineering) exists. Adjacent concerns: `RES.IO.remote` (HTTP/CDN IO with cache and offline policy), `RES.IO.errors` (retries) and `BLD.REL.on-demand` (background assets).\n- Ruling rationale: The HTTPS/TLS client, the browser transports, the crypto primitives with CSPRNG and the TLS/certificate policy each now have exactly one owner and a contract path. Putting the client in the PAL is technically sound, because console certification requires the platform network stacks. Keeping primitives in a foundation (L1) skill, with policy owned by security, follows the §4 policy-vs-implementation pattern. Non-binding note: the HTTP client is a clause inside a capability about processes and pipes, and proxies and certificate pinning are not named anywhere. Splitting it into its own `PLAT.PAL.http` line would help phase-2 research seeding, but the ownership gap the critic raised is closed.\n\n### K-FUTURE-10 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: xr-runtime now owns `PLAT.XR.passthrough` (E), `PLAT.XR.scene` (M, contributors collision-detection and navigation-pathfinding), `PLAT.XR.anchors` (M, contributors network-architect and persistence-save), `PLAT.XR.depth-occlusion` (M, contributor render-architect), `PLAT.XR.layers` (E), `PLAT.XR.gaze-input` (M, contributor input-system), `PLAT.XR.light-estimation` (M) and `PLAT.XR.mobile-ar`. The radar entry \"Mixed reality & spatial computing\" carries an owner, a revisit trigger and a VR-only fallback. `C-XRVIEW` covers compositor layers. xr-runtime owns 13 capabilities, below `MAX_CAPS_SKILL` = 30.\n- Ruling rationale: Every capability the critic proposed exists with the proposed owner and contributors, and the anchor-sharing boundary with networking is expressed through the contributor. The critic made the split conditional on exceeding the size norm, and xr-runtime does not exceed it. Non-binding note: the xr-runtime `purpose` and `expertise` still describe VR only (OpenXR, stereo rendering, motion-to-photon). They should gain scene understanding, spatial anchors and passthrough compositing, so that G2 can judge the \"distinct literature load\" the builder names as the split trigger.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-4 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-VIEW` (L2, owner spatial-transforms) summary: \"view sources (gameplay camera, cinematic, editor, photo mode, XR pose) with priority/blend stack and shake/FOV channels, per-local-player views, view origin for LWC rebasing; derived listener and streaming-source signals\". `WLD.SPACE.views` has contributors gameplay-systems-toolkit, cinematics-sequencer, xr-runtime and render-architect. `C-VIEW` is consumed by render-architect, cinematics-sequencer, xr-runtime, world-architect, audio-architect, gameplay-systems-toolkit and world-editor-viewport, and optionally by gameplay-architect. `C-XRVIEW` and `C-SIGNIF` require `C-VIEW`. `GAM.SYS.camera` now reads \"(C-VIEW sources)\". `AUD.ARCH.listeners` (\"Multiple listeners & split-screen mixing\") exists, and `C-AUDIO` now covers multiple listeners. Accessibility reaches the channels through `UI.A11Y.motion` and the process contract `C-A11Y`, which gameplay-systems-toolkit and cinematics-sequencer consume.\n- Ruling rationale: The arbitration layer exists with one owner, and every sink the critic listed consumes it. Placing it at L2 below gameplay is technically better than a gameplay-owned `C-CAMERA`, because the editor, XR and cinematics must drive views in configurations without the gameplay framework. Non-binding note: the spatial-transforms `purpose` and `expertise` do not yet mention view arbitration or camera blending.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-15 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `C-AIAGENT` exists (L4, owner ai-behavior-perception; \"budget, timeout, moderation and authored fallback\"), and gameplay-architect consumes it as `C-AIAGENT?`. `GAM.AI.learned` and `GAM.AI.llm` are now M and reference `C-AIAGENT`. The `GAM.AI.llm` line also references `XC.SEC.genai`. ai-behavior-perception consumes `C-ML?` and `C-LIVE?`. `C-LIVE` covers \"generative/inference service calls with quotas\". The following guardrail capabilities exist: `XC.SEC.genai` (with PII), `QA.CERT.genai`, `PLAT.SVC.genai-boundary` and `PLAT.SVC.moderation`. Runtime TTS is `AUD.CONTENT.speech`. The radar entry has an owner, a revisit trigger and a fallback. Still missing:\n  - No capability or contributor covers localization or culturalization of runtime-generated text. localization-i18n appears on no genai-related capability.\n  - Nothing covers persistence of conversational memory. ai-behavior-perception does not consume `C-SAVE?`, and no capability names this concern.\n  - ai-behavior-perception `expertise` is still [\"game AI\", \"utility theory\", \"HTN planning\"], with no ML or LLM competence.\n  - `check.py` has no rule for the §7 claim that non-established techniques are \"reached through optional contracts\". The radar check (lines 309–320) verifies that non-E capabilities are tracked and have a revisit trigger. It does not check fallback, and it does not check reachability.\n- Ruling rationale: The core extension point and the moderation, budget and fallback requirements from 06 A12 are now in place. However, the critic listed specific concerns as having no owner, and two of them still have none: localization of generated text and persistence of conversational memory (which also carries PII). The competence gap in the skill's expertise also remains. The builder's statement that the radar rule replaces the proposed check is technically wrong. The radar rule tests tracking. The critic's rule tests dependency optionality, which the radar rule never examines. So §7's reachability claim remains unchecked.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add localization-i18n as a contributor to `GAM.AI.llm` or `XC.SEC.genai`. Alternatively, add `UI.LOC.generated` (\"Locale/culturalization of runtime-generated text\"), owner localization-i18n, contributor ai-behavior-perception.\n  2. Add `C-SAVE?` to ai-behavior-perception `consumes`, and add persistence-save as a contributor to `GAM.AI.llm`. Name the behavior \"conversational memory persistence and retention\", or cover it in `C-AIAGENT`'s summary.\n  3. Add \"ML policy/LLM integration\" to ai-behavior-perception `expertise`.\n  4. Close the §7 gap in one of two ways. Either add a check.py rule that a skill owning an M, X or S capability consumes every ML or service contract (`C-ML`, `C-MLGPU`, `C-RT`, `C-LIVE`) only as optional `?`, or require radar entries for M, X and S capabilities to include `fallback`. Otherwise, reword §7 so that it does not claim a check that does not exist.\n\n### K-LEGACY-7 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: The §6 row now reads \"submission strategy is chosen per hardware tier by ADR ... CPU-culled batched path is first-class on TBDR/lite3d. 2D draws through batched draw lists by design\". `RND.ARCH.submission-strategy` exists (render-architect, contributors geometry-pipeline and platform-mobile, \"TBDR suitability\"). `RND.GEO.cpu-submission` exists (\"first-class tier path (own budgets and tests)\", contributor platform-mobile). `RND.GEO.visbuffer` requires a TBDR suitability ADR. geometry-pipeline has profiles [lite3d, std3d], and its `purpose` names both paths. Legacy patterns L12 and L27 are qualified. Two items are unchanged. geometry-pipeline `expertise` is still [\"GPU culling\", \"mesh shaders\", \"ExecuteIndirect\"]. The K-RENDER critic check is still the unqualified \"no draw-call-centric or DX11-era assumptions\".\n- Ruling rationale: Keeping both paths in one skill because they serve one contract (`C-INSTANCES`) is a legitimate choice, and the per-tier decision now sits with render-architect. However, the critic's stated root cause was that the mobile path is \"owned by an agent whose expertise and critic checks push the other way\". The builder's decision to keep the same owner is sound only if that owner's competence covers the CPU path. It does not, and the domain critic's check can still be read as flagging the now first-class path.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add \"CPU culling & instanced batching\" and \"TBDR/mobile GPU submission best practice (Mali/Adreno/Apple)\" to geometry-pipeline `expertise`.\n  2. Change the K-RENDER check to \"no draw-call-centric or DX11-era assumptions on capable tiers; CPU-batched submission on TBDR/lite3d follows RND.ARCH.submission-strategy\".\n\n### K-PERF-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: The new skill runtime-scalability (expert, parent core-runtime-architect, kind runtime, profiles all) owns `CORE.SCALE.governor` (contributors frame-orchestration, platform-mobile, performance-architect), `CORE.SCALE.actuators` (\"dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, workers\"), `CORE.SCALE.profiles` and `CORE.SCALE.autodetect`. It provides `C-SCALE` (L1, universal runtime; \"actuator registration and governor control signals\"), so every runtime actuator owner reaches it implicitly. It consumes `C-INSTR` for frame and GPU timings and `C-PAL` for thermal and memory-pressure events. `PRF.METH.scalability` is narrowed to policy. performance-architect is `kind: process`.\n- Ruling rationale: One closed-loop governor with a single actuator registry now exists, and it drives the actuators the critic named. The builder's owner change is required, not a preference: performance-architect is now a process skill, and process skills cannot own runtime code. The residual split between the thermal loop and `PLAT.MOB.thermal` is ruled on under K-SYSTEMS-13.\n\n### K-PROD-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: The `C-ORCH` summary now contains \"write sets by module convention (src/<skill-id>/, tools/<skill-id>/; tests owned by the test-owning skill); shared-file protocol; contributor semantics (review + change request, never direct writes)\". `ARCH.ORG.write-sets` exists (program-orchestration, contributor build-system-toolchains). The shared hotspots the critic named have owners:\n  - `ARCH.STRUCT.registration`: declarative self-registration with \"no central tables\", which removes most shared registration files.\n  - `RND.SHADER.interop`: shared shader headers, owned by shader-system.\n  - `BLD.SYS.codegen`: codegen integration.\n- Ruling rationale: Write sets keyed by skill id are disjoint by construction, because check.py enforces unique skill ids. That delivers the proof the critic asked for without per-skill globs. The contributor semantics now answer the critic's question directly. Deferring explicit globs until code exists (M0) is a legitimate choice with no material risk, because the convention already makes write sets unambiguous.\n\n### K-PROD-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: All 143 skills have a `workstream`, with 15 values. `ARCH.ORG.staffing` (\"co-hosting of skills per organization scale (by workstream)\") and `ARCH.GOV.process-tiers` both exist. §5 line 79 describes organizational scale-down. The check.py configuration report now counts \"build skills\" only (runtime and tool) and excludes the 22 process skills: indie-2d-client has 75 build skills and minimal-client has 66.\n- Ruling rationale: Organizational scale-down and governance weight are now owned capabilities. The workstream field serves as the co-hosting key that the critic's `host_group` would have provided. Runtime membership is reported separately from process membership. Defining the solo, small and large tier tables is the deliverable of `ARCH.ORG.staffing`, so deferring `host_group` to that capability is a legitimate choice.\n\n### K-QUALITY-6 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `QA.STRAT.contracts` is narrowed to \"Contract-test framework & consumer-driven test policy\". `C-TEST` now includes \"conformance suites owned by contract owners with consumer-driven additions\". All 86 non-P contracts carry `\"conformance\": true`. However, `scripts/check.py` and `scripts/model.py` contain no reference to `conformance` (grep returns nothing). The flag is therefore not checked, although the disposition says \"(checked)\". `C-ORCH` contains no rule that a provider change must pass the consumer-contributed tests.\n- Ruling rationale: Ownership is resolved in substance, and a separate owner field would indeed be redundant. But one of the two claimed changes is missing. With no guard, a flag that is always `true` carries no information, and a new contract can omit it silently. The critic's point about replaceability also depends on a provider change being gated by consumer-driven tests, and that gating rule is not stated.\n- Required change:\n  1. In `check.py`, add `E(f\"code contract {cid} has no conformance suite\")` for every contract with layer ≠ \"P\" that lacks `conformance: true`. Add a mutation case for it to `selftest()`.\n  2. Extend the `C-TEST` or `C-ORCH` summary with \"a provider change merges only when the contract's conformance suite, including consumer-contributed cases, passes\".\n\n### K-RENDER-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: texture-streaming-vt has profiles [\"all\"]. `RND.TEX.formats` and `RND.TEX.mip-streaming` carry no profile gate. `RND.TEX.vt` and `RND.TEX.feedback` are gated to std3d, and `RND.TEX.ntc` to aaa. The min2d description is now \"Small 2D games (sprite/tile renderer, 2D physics)\" and no longer claims 2.5D. The new base profile lite3d (\"mobile, portable, 2.5D, stylized; CPU-submitted rendering tier\") includes geometry-pipeline with `RND.GEO.cpu-submission`, direct-lighting-shadows and translucency-decals. `PHY.DYN.dof-lock` (\"Planar / DOF-locked simulation (2.5D)\") belongs to rigid-body-dynamics, which has the lite3d profile. check.py configuration closure passes.\n- Ruling rationale: The critic accepted both alternatives the builder combined: drop 2.5D from min2d and give it a proper profile, and give basic textures an owner in every profile with VT as a tier. Capability-level profile gating reaches the same scale-down as splitting the skill. min2d now carries only formats and mip streaming, not VT.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-13 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `CORE.SCALE.governor` and `CORE.SCALE.actuators` exist under runtime-scalability. `PLAT.PAL.power` (\"Power, thermal & performance-hint APIs\", platform-architect, contributor runtime-scalability) exists. The runtime-scalability `expertise` includes ADPF and Apple thermal state. Two gaps remain:\n  - `PLAT.MOB.thermal` still reads \"Thermal & power adaptivity\". It is owned by platform-mobile, a runtime skill whose `purpose` still says \"thermal/power adaptivity\".\n  - The registered actuators are \"dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, workers\". Frame-rate cap and refresh-rate selection are not among them, and neither is P/E-core placement. Frame-rate and refresh-rate selection live in `PLAT.MOB.frame-pacing` and CORE.FRAME, and placement lives in `CORE.JOBS.priorities`. job-system-task-graph is not a contributor to `CORE.SCALE.actuators`.\n- Ruling rationale: The owner choice is sound: one skill holds both the registry and the loop. The critic's concrete failure mode was \"dynres, job placement and thermal handling run three uncoordinated control loops\". The data still gives a second runtime skill a capability worded as thermal adaptation, so two agents can each legitimately build a thermal control loop. The frame-rate cap, the primary thermal actuator in ADPF guidance and on handhelds, and P/E placement, both of which the critic named, are not registered as governor actuators.\n- Required change:\n  1. Rename `PLAT.MOB.thermal` to \"Mobile thermal/power signal backends (ADPF thermal headroom, performance-hint sessions) feeding CORE.SCALE.governor; no independent control loop\", and update the platform-mobile `purpose` to match.\n  2. Change `CORE.SCALE.actuators` to \"(frame-rate cap/refresh selection, dynres, significance, VFX, animation rate, worker count & P/E placement)\". Add frame-orchestration, platform-mobile and job-system-task-graph as contributors.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-BUDGET` is now layer \"P\", universal \"all\", owner performance-architect (\"Budget numbers per hardware tier × configuration × refresh class ...; perf gate thresholds\"). `C-SCALE` is L1, universal runtime, owner runtime-scalability, requires `C-CFG`. runtime-scalability's parent is core-runtime-architect, and it is placed in milestone M0. performance-architect has `kind: process` and `targets: []`. check.py reports no foundation cycle.\n- Ruling rationale: The split is implemented as the critic proposed. Assigning `C-SCALE` to a dedicated expert under core-runtime-architect instead of the lead is consistent with the lead/expert structure. It also keeps the governor logic from K-PERF-8 and K-SYSTEMS-13 with the contract owner.\n\n### K-TOOLS-12 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `ED.WORLD.blockout` (\"Blockout & in-editor modeling (primitives, booleans, UVs)\", world-editor-viewport, contributor asset-cook-processors) exists. `ED.WORLD.ld-utilities` exists. `CNT.COOK.geometry-ops` (\"Mesh boolean, remesh & UV-unwrap operations for processors and blockout\", asset-cook-processors, contributor world-editor-viewport) exists. However, world-editor-viewport consumes [\"C-EDCMD\", \"C-EDHOST\", \"C-WORLD\", \"C-RSCENE\", \"C-SPATIAL\", \"C-VIEW\"] and has empty `tool_consumes`. asset-cook-processors `provides` nothing, and it plugs into `C-COOK`, which world-editor-viewport does not consume. The `C-MATH` booleans (`CORE.MATH.compgeo`) are not consumed by world-editor-viewport either. world-editor-viewport `purpose` and `expertise` (\"3D manipulation UX\", \"large-world editing\") do not mention blockout or modeling.\n- Ruling rationale: The missing workflows now exist as capabilities, and deferring a separate mesh-modeling skill is legitimate. But the builder's placement splits one interactive feature across two skills with no contract between them. The blockout tool cannot call the geometry operations it depends on except by reaching into another skill's internals, which the contracts rule forbids (`contracts.json` `_doc`: \"Skills depend on contracts, never directly on another skill's internals\"). check.py does not detect this, because contributor links are not dependencies.\n- Required change:\n  1. Give world-editor-viewport a contract path to the geometry operations. One option: add `C-COOK` to its `tool_consumes` and extend the `C-COOK` summary with \"interactive, editor-invoked geometry operations (boolean/remesh/UV)\", building on the existing \"per-asset on-demand invocation\". The other option: have world-editor-viewport consume `C-MATH` and reword `CNT.COOK.geometry-ops` so that the interactive kernels are `CORE.MATH.compgeo`.\n  2. Add blockout and in-editor modeling to world-editor-viewport `purpose`, and add \"mesh editing / geometry processing\" to its `expertise`.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:** 13 findings adjudicated: 8 UPHOLD (K-COMPLETE-3, K-FUTURE-10, K-GAMEPLAY-4, K-PERF-8, K-PROD-2, K-PROD-8, K-RENDER-7, K-SYSTEMS-14), 5 OVERTURN (K-GAMEPLAY-15, K-LEGACY-7, K-QUALITY-6, K-SYSTEMS-13, K-TOOLS-12).\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfY32UcAqoL6vHXLy8QQz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"64a48e05-19be-4a76-82e8-ecec237f0991","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:21:33.842Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
